\chapter{Building Trees: \code{leaf<>} and \code{node<>}}
\label{ch:leaf-and-node}

\section{Why factories and not constructors}

\chapref{ch:astnode} described \api{ASTNode} as a value type with three
constructors. Those constructors are usable, but they are not ergonomic.
Every call site would have to name the payload type explicitly, and every
tag would have to be spelled as a string literal:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Constructing nodes directly: correct, but noisy.},label={lst:direct-ctors}]
dsl::ASTNode n1 ("num", std::string ("42"));
dsl::ASTNode n2 ("add", std::vector<dsl::ASTNode>{ n1, n1 });
\end{lstlisting}

The tag in that code is a runtime string, which means the compiler cannot
check it, cannot dispatch on it, and cannot fold two nodes with the same
tag into one type. \DSLtk{} therefore provides a small family of factory
functions whose \emph{template parameter} is the tag and whose
\emph{arguments} are the payload. The tag becomes a compile-time constant;
the payload is ordinary run-time data.

The four factories occupy lines 6662--6707 of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}. They are
free functions in namespace \dslns, not members of \api{ASTNode} and not
members of any feature mixin:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{5.6cm}C{1.0cm}L{6.6cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Overload} & \textbf{Line} & \textbf{Behaviour} \\
\midrule
\code{leaf<Tag>(T&& val)}
  & 6662
  & Generic. Streams \code{val} into an \code{ostringstream} and stores the
    resulting \emph{text}. Accepts anything with an
    \code{operator<<}. \\
\code{leaf<Tag>(std::string_view val)}
  & 6672
  & Stores the string contents directly, skipping the stream. \\
\code{leaf<Tag>(const char* val)}
  & 6680
  & Stores the C string directly, skipping the stream. \\
\code{node<Tag>(Children&&... children)}
  & 6698
  & Builds an inner node. The children are copied or moved, in argument
    order, into a \code{std::vector<ASTNode>}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The four AST factories. \code{Tag} is a \api{FixedString}
non-type template parameter in every case.}
\label{tab:leaf-node-overloads}
\end{table}

\section{A worked example}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Building a small expression tree with the factories.},label={lst:leaf-node-demo}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>
#include <vector>

// A DSL that mixes in the AST feature, so that make_leaf/make_node are
// available as static members.
struct Calc : dsl::DSL<Calc, dsl::AST>
{
};

int main ()
{
  using dsl::ASTNode;

  // leaf<> renders its argument through an ostringstream, so the value
  // stored in the node is the *text* "42".
  ASTNode n = dsl::leaf<"num"> (42);
  std::cout << n.dump () << '\n';
  std::cout << "textual value: " << std::boolalpha << (n.value () == "42")
            << '\n';

  // The string overloads skip the stream but store the same text.
  ASTNode a = dsl::leaf<"s"> (std::string_view ("hello"));
  ASTNode b = dsl::leaf<"s"> ("hello");
  ASTNode c = dsl::leaf<"s"> (std::string ("hello"));
  std::cout << a.dump () << ' ' << b.dump () << ' ' << c.dump () << '\n';
  std::cout << "all equal: " << (a == b && b == c) << '\n';

  // node<> keeps its children in the order they were written.
  ASTNode add = dsl::node<"add"> (dsl::leaf<"x"> (1), dsl::leaf<"y"> (2));
  std::cout << add.dump () << '\n';

  // Arity is not checked: node<"f"> accepts any number of children.
  ASTNode none = dsl::node<"f"> ();
  ASTNode three = dsl::node<"f"> (dsl::leaf<"a"> (1),
                                  dsl::leaf<"b"> (2),
                                  dsl::leaf<"c"> (3));
  std::cout << none.dump () << ' ' << three.dump () << '\n';

  // Building a tree from a flat vector: use the inner-node constructor
  // directly, because node<> takes its children as a variadic argument list.
  std::vector<ASTNode> kids;
  kids.push_back (dsl::leaf<"num"> (10));
  kids.push_back (dsl::leaf<"num"> (20));
  ASTNode built{ "add", std::move (kids) };
  std::cout << built.dump () << '\n';

  // The AST feature's static wrappers have the same signatures.
  ASTNode m = Calc::make_node<"mul"> (Calc::make_leaf<"num"> (6),
                                      Calc::make_leaf<"num"> (7));
  std::cout << m.dump () << '\n';
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:leaf-node-demo}.},label={lst:leaf-node-demo-out}]
(num 42)
textual value: true
(s hello) (s hello) (s hello)
all equal: true
(add (x 1) (y 2))
(f) (f (a 1) (b 2) (c 3))
(add (num 10) (num 20))
(mul (num 6) (num 7))
\end{lstlisting}

\section{What the tag does, and what it does not do}

The \code{Tag} template parameter of every factory is a
\api{FixedString}, the compile-time string holder described in
\chapref{ch:fixed-string}. A \api{FixedString} is a literal type: it can be
used as a non-type template parameter, and two \code{FixedString}s with the
same characters are the same \emph{type} only when their lengths agree.

What happens to that compile-time tag is the crux of this chapter.
\code{leaf} and \code{node} both pass \code{Tag.view()} --- a
\code{std::string_view} over the \api{FixedString}'s character array --- to
the \api{ASTNode} constructor, which \emph{copies} it into the node's
\code{std::string tag_} member. The node does not remember the
\api{FixedString}; it keeps a runtime string.

\begin{notebox}[The compile-time tag does not survive into the type system]
\code{leaf<"a">(1)} and \code{leaf<"b">(1)} have the same type --- both are
\code{ASTNode} --- and they compare equal only if their tag strings match,
which they do not. There is no way to recover the tag of an
\api{ASTNode} at compile time, no way to dispatch on it statically, and no
way to write an overload set that is selected by it. The compile-time tag
is a convenience for the \emph{caller}, not a guarantee about the
\emph{node}.
\end{notebox}

This is the single most important thing to understand about the AST
subsystem, and it explains the existence of two other pieces of
\DSLtk{}. The compile-time schema of \chapref{ch:visitors} ---
\api{ast_schema} --- exists precisely
because \api{ASTNode} cannot carry a tag in its type; and the
\api{RewriteRule} of \chapref{ch:rewrite} accepts a tag as a template
parameter for the same reason, so that two rules with different names are
different types even though the engine never looks at their names.

A second consequence of the copy is lifetime safety. \code{Tag.view()}
points into the \api{FixedString}'s static storage, but the node copies the
characters into its own \code{std::string}, so a node outlives nothing and
depends on nothing.

A third consequence is that trees are not constant expressions.
\api{ASTNode} is not a literal type: it has a user-provided constructor,
and \code{leaf} allocates an \code{ostringstream}. The error is explicit:
\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Trees cannot be built in a constant expression.},label={lst:not-constexpr}]
constexpr dsl::ASTNode n = dsl::leaf<"x"> (1);
// error: the type 'const dsl::ASTNode' of 'constexpr' variable 'n' is not literal
// error: 'dsl::ASTNode' is not literal because:
// error: it is not an aggregate, does not have a trivial default constructor,
// error: and has no 'constexpr' constructor that is not a copy or move constructor
\end{lstlisting}

The compile-time tag therefore buys compile-time \emph{checking} of the tag
and nothing more. The tree itself is a run-time object.

It is worth adding one detail about the \api{FixedString} itself, because
it is easy to assume more than is there. The length of the string is part
of the type: \code{FixedString<4>} and \code{FixedString<5>} are different
types even if the characters agree, so \code{leaf<"ab">} and
\code{leaf<"abc">} instantiate different template specialisations even
though both produce a node whose runtime tag is the text between the
quotes. The length distinction exists because a class type with a
character array member cannot be a non-type template parameter unless its
size is known at compile time. What the length does \emph{not} do is give
the node a compile-time tag; the node stores a \code{std::string}, and
\code{std::string} has no compile-time size.

\section{Overload resolution between the three \code{leaf} overloads}

Because \code{leaf} is overloaded on the type of its second parameter, the
choice of overload is a matter of some subtlety. The measured behaviour of
the shipped header is as follows.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{6.0cm}L{4.4cm}L{2.8cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Argument type} & \textbf{Overload selected} & \textbf{Stored text} \\
\midrule
\code{int}, \code{double}, \code{bool}
  & generic (\code{T&&})
  & the streamed representation \\
\code{std::string} (lvalue or rvalue)
  & generic (\code{T&&})
  & the string contents \\
\code{std::string_view}
  & the \code{string_view} overload
  & the view's contents \\
\code{const char*}
  & the \code{const char*} overload
  & the C string \\
a string literal \code{"hi"}
  & the \code{const char*} overload
  & the literal's characters \\
\code{const char[N]} (lvalue)
  & the \code{const char*} overload
  & the array's characters \\
\code{char[N]} (lvalue)
  & generic (\code{T&&})
  & the streamed representation \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Which \code{leaf} overload wins, and what it stores. The stored
text is the same in every case; only the mechanism differs.}
\label{tab:leaf-overload-resolution}
\end{table}

Two observations are worth drawing out. First, the specialised overloads
are \emph{optimisations only}. The header's comment at line 6671 says as
much: the \code{string_view} overload "avoids extra stream". For every
argument type in the table, the resulting node has the same tag and the
same text, so the two nodes compare equal. A user therefore never has to
think about which overload was chosen --- only about what text they want.

Second, the selection is not simply "the most specific type wins". The
generic overload takes its argument by forwarding reference, so it is an
exact match for every type, including arrays. A \code{char[N]} lvalue
selects the generic overload, because binding \code{char(&)[N]} is an
identity match whereas converting to \code{const char*} additionally needs a
qualification adjustment. A \code{const char[N]} lvalue has no such
problem and selects the \code{const char*} overload, and a string literal
behaves the same way.

\section{How \code{node<>} stores its children}

The inner-node factory is short, and every line of it matters:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The \code{node} factory, verbatim from the header.},label={lst:node-impl}]
template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
  requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
ASTNode
node (Children &&...children)
{
  std::vector<ASTNode> v;
  v.reserve (sizeof...(children));
  (v.emplace_back (std::forward<Children> (children)), ...);
  return ASTNode{ Tag.view (), std::move (v) };
}
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{reserve} call sizes the vector exactly once, before any element
exists, so the \code{emplace_back} calls never reallocate. The fold
expression is a unary right fold over the comma operator, which means the
elements are constructed in argument order --- the first argument becomes
child~0, the second becomes child~1, and so on. The vector is then moved
into the node, so the children are relocated rather than copied a second
time.

Because the parameter is a forwarding reference, an rvalue child is
\emph{moved} into the vector and an lvalue child is \emph{copied}. The
difference matters: constructing
\code{node<"add">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1), dsl::leaf<"num">(2))} moves two
temporary nodes and copies nothing, whereas reusing a named node copies it.

The \code{requires} clause asks only that each \code{Children} type be
convertible to \api{ASTNode}. In practice this admits \api{ASTNode} itself
and references to it, and nothing else. In particular, a
\code{std::vector<ASTNode>} is \emph{not} accepted:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A vector of children is rejected by the constraint.},label={lst:node-vector-rejected}]
std::vector<dsl::ASTNode> v;
auto n = dsl::node<"f"> (v);
// error: no matching function for call to 'node<"f">(std::vector<dsl::ASTNode>&)'
// note: constraints not satisfied
\end{lstlisting}

A note on the fold. \code{(v.emplace_back (...), ...)} is a unary right
fold over the comma operator: the pack expands to
\code{(e1, e2, e3)}. The comma operator sequences its operands, so the
fold evaluates the arguments strictly left to right. That is what makes
the order of the children well defined; without it, the order of the
arguments in a variadic call would be unspecified and the tree would be
ambiguous.

\section{The \code{AST} feature and its static wrappers}

The feature tag \api{dsl::AST} is declared at line 6723. It contributes a
mixin with exactly two static members:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{6.2cm}C{1.0cm}L{6.0cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Member} & \textbf{Line} & \textbf{Behaviour} \\
\midrule
\code{make_leaf<Tag>(T&& val)}
  & 6730
  & Forwards to \code{dsl::leaf<Tag>}. Same signature, same constraint
    (none), same result. \\
\code{make_node<Tag>(Children&&...)}
  & 6740
  & Forwards to \code{dsl::node<Tag>}. Same signature, same
    \code{convertible_to} constraint, same result. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The \code{AST} feature mixin. Both members are static and are
inherited by the DSL class.}
\label{tab:ast-feature-mixin}
\end{table}

The wrappers exist so that a DSL can write its own \code{make_leaf} and
\code{make_node} instead of the fully qualified \code{dsl::leaf} and
\code{dsl::node}. Because the mixin is a base class
of \api{dsl::DSL} and hence of the user's class, the static members are
inherited and reachable through the derived name. They add nothing
behavioural: no state, no caching, no validation. A user who prefers the
free functions can ignore the feature entirely --- \code{dsl::leaf} and
\code{dsl::node} do not require any mixin to be present.

It is worth noting what the mixin does \emph{not} do. It does not
constrain the derived class to define anything, and it does not use the
\code{Derived} parameter at all: the mixin is generic over \code{Derived}
purely to fit the feature-mixin protocol of \chapref{ch:feature-tags}. A
consequence is that the two static members can be called without ever
instantiating a DSL object; \code{MyDSL::make_leaf<"x">(1)} is a static
call, and there is no \code{this} to speak of.

\section{The factories and the rest of the toolkit}

The four factories are the only sanctioned way to build a node from
scratch, but they are not the only way to obtain one. Three other parts of
\DSLtk{} produce \api{ASTNode} values, and it is worth knowing how they
relate to the factories.

The rewrite machinery of \chapref{ch:rewrite} produces nodes with the
factories, in the transformer of a rule. A rule that rewrites
\code{add(num 1, num 2)} into a folded constant will typically return
\code{dsl::leaf<"num">(3)}; a rule that removes a wrapper will typically
return \code{n.child(0)}, that is, a node it did not build at all. Because
a transformer returns an \api{ASTNode} \emph{by value}, the engine moves
the replacement into place, and the subtree beneath the replacement is
shared with the tree the rule was handed --- which, since the node holds
its children by value, means it is copied. This is the copy cost of
\chapref{ch:astnode} showing up again, and it is the reason a rewrite
engine built on value semantics is not free.

The S-expression reader of \chapref{ch:sexpressions} produces nodes
through the \api{ASTNode} constructors directly rather than through
\code{leaf} and \code{node}, because the tag of a node read from a
S-expression is not known at compile time. The two-argument leaf
constructor is exactly the right tool there: the tag is a runtime string
and the value is a runtime string. The factories and the constructors are
complementary, not competing.

Finally, the visitor subsystem of \chapref{ch:visitors} is built on a
completely separate notion of a schema. \api{ast_schema} builds a
\emph{compile-time} tree whose node types are \api{ASTSchema} and whose tags
are template parameters; \api{ASTVisitor} then dispatches on the runtime
tag of an \api{ASTNode} by comparing it against the tags recorded in that
compile-time tree. In other words, the visitors recover exactly the
type-level tag information that \code{leaf} and \code{node} discard. The
two mechanisms are complementary in the same way: the factories make
construction pleasant, and the schema makes dispatch exhaustive.

\section{Construction cost}

Every factory allocates. \code{leaf} constructs an
\code{std::ostringstream}, writes into it, and copies the result out as a
\code{std::string}; the stream's internal buffer is then released. For a
tree with many leaves --- and a source file with many tokens has many
leaves --- this is the dominant cost of parsing. A front end that cares
about throughput should prefer the \code{string_view} or \code{const char*}
overload for text leaves, which allocate exactly one string and nothing
else, and should reserve its child vectors when the arity is known.

\code{node} is cheaper per child: it allocates one vector, reserves
exactly the right amount of space, and moves each child into place. The
one copy that does happen is the one the caller asked for by passing an
lvalue.

