\section{Recipes}

\paragraph{Heterogeneous children.}
Nothing requires the children of a node to share a tag or a shape. A node
tagged \code{"call"} may hold a leaf tagged \code{"fn"}, a leaf tagged
\code{"arg"}, and another inner node, in any combination. Each child
carries its own tag, and \api{size()} reports the arity. A DSL that wants
homogeneous children must enforce that itself, by convention or by a
predicate.

\paragraph{Building from a flat vector.}
\code{node<>} takes a variadic argument list, so it cannot consume a
vector of children directly. The inner-node constructor can:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Assembling a node from a vector of children.},label={lst:build-from-vector}]
std::vector<dsl::ASTNode> kids;
kids.push_back (dsl::leaf<"num"> (10));
kids.push_back (dsl::leaf<"num"> (20));
dsl::ASTNode sum{ "add", std::move (kids) };   // inner node
\end{lstlisting}

Note that the second argument is \emph{moved}, so the vector is left empty
afterwards. Passing it by copy would double the work, since the vector's
copy constructor copies every child.

\paragraph{Arity is not checked.}
\code{node<"f">()} is legal and produces an inner node with no children,
which dumps as \code{(f)}. \code{node<"f">(a, b, c)} is equally legal. The
constraint on \code{node<>} says nothing about how many children there are.
A DSL that cares about arity must check it with \api{size()} --- in a rule
predicate, in a visitor handler, or immediately after construction.

\paragraph{Appending to a finished node.}
Because \code{children()} returns a mutable reference, a node can be
extended after the fact with \code{node.children().push_back (child)}.
Nothing checks that the result is still a sensible node: appending to a
leaf silently turns a leaf into a leaf with children, which
\chapref{ch:astnode} describes as a broken invariant. Appending is
convenient in a parser that discovers a child late; it is dangerous in a
library that promises well-formed trees.

\section{Building a tree from a flat token list}

The most common construction problem in practice is the inverse of what
these factories make comfortable: a parser has produced a flat sequence of
tokens and must turn it into a tree. \code{node<>} does not help with that
directly, because it wants its children as separate arguments. The
following scheme does.

\begin{pseudocode}[Building a tree from a flat token list]
\textbf{function} build (tokens, pos, depth)\\
\quad \textbf{if} pos \code{>=} length (tokens) \textbf{then return} null\\
\quad head \code{<-} tokens [pos]\\
\quad \textbf{if} head is an opening token \textbf{then}\\
\qquad pos \code{<-} pos + 1\\
\qquad children \code{<-} empty list\\
\qquad \textbf{while} tokens [pos] is not a closing token \textbf{do}\\
\qquad\quad child \code{<-} build (tokens, pos, depth + 1)\\
\qquad\quad append child to children\\
\qquad pos \code{<-} pos + 1\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{node<"list">} (children...)\\
\quad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{leaf<"atom">} (head)
\end{pseudocode}

The scheme is a shift/reduce parser in miniature: the token stream is the
input, the call stack is the recursion, and the tree is the output. The
important detail is that the recursion produces \emph{children}, not a flat
list, and that the variadic factory wants children as arguments --- so the
natural implementation collects them into a \code{std::vector<ASTNode>} and
then hands that vector to the inner-node constructor, exactly as in the
recipe above. The alternative, which is more convenient when the arity is
known statically, is to build the children expression directly in the
recursive call.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\paragraph{The tag is not part of the node's type.}
Already stated, but worth repeating because it is the most common source of
surprise. Two nodes with different tags have the same C++ type, so
\code{std::variant} dispatch, overload resolution, and template specialisation
are all unavailable on \api{ASTNode}. Only the runtime string is
discriminating.

\paragraph{The value is always text.}
\code{leaf<"num">(42)} stores the string \code{"42"}, not the integer. A
rule that constant-folds arithmetic must parse the text back --- with
\code{std::stoi}, \code{std::from_chars}, or a hand-written scanner --- and
must decide what to do with text that is not a number. There is no
numeric payload in \api{ASTNode}, and no way to attach one.

\paragraph{Values are not quoted.}
\code{leaf<"s">("a b")} produces \code{(s a b)}, which is ambiguous when
read back. If a value may contain whitespace or parentheses, the caller
must quote it itself before storing it, or use the \api{SExpr} machinery of
\chapref{ch:sexpressions}, which has a documented escalation rule.

\paragraph{No arity or shape checking.}
Nothing in the factories validates that a node tagged \code{"add"} has two
children, or that a node tagged \code{"num"} is a leaf. Such checks belong
in predicates and visitors.

\paragraph{No constness in the payload.}
\code{leaf<Tag>(const char* val)} reads the C string but does not copy it
into the caller's storage; the node does copy it into \code{value_}, so
the result is independent of the caller's buffer afterwards. Passing a
pointer to a buffer that is later modified is nevertheless a bug in the
caller's code, not in the factory's.

\paragraph{Exception safety.}
\code{leaf} allocates a stream; if the stream's insertion throws --- a
\code{std::bad_alloc}, or a user-defined \code{operator<<} that throws ---
the exception propagates and no node is produced. \code{node} is stronger:
the vector is a local, so a throwing copy or move of a child unwinds
cleanly and destroys the already-constructed children.

\paragraph{No factory for an existing tree.}
There is no \code{clone}, \code{with_tag}, or \code{with_value}. To
change a node's tag or a leaf's text, a caller must construct a new node
and move the children across by hand.

\paragraph{Mixing the free functions and the mixin.}
\code{dsl::leaf} and \code{MyDSL::make_leaf} are interchangeable; there
is no interaction between them and no state to initialise. A user may mix
and match freely within a single program.

\section{Closing}

The four factories of \chapref{ch:leaf-and-node} are a thin layer over the
three constructors of \chapref{ch:astnode}, and their whole contribution
is to move the tag from the run-time world into the compile-time one. What
that buys is a checkable tag at the call site; what it costs is that the
node itself remains untagged at the type level, so that the tag must be
matched as a string everywhere downstream.

The two facts a reader should carry forward are that the value of a leaf
is always \emph{text} --- \code{leaf<"num">(42)} stores \code{"42"}, not
\code{42} --- and that a node's arity, shape, and homogeneity are
conventions that the factories neither enforce nor record. Everything in
the next two chapters works with those conventions rather than around
them: \chapref{ch:traversal} walks the trees these factories build, and
\chapref{ch:rewrite} and \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} transform them.
